  "chem-aqa-8462-2025-p2f::09.7": "C₃H₈ + 5 O₂ → <strong>3</strong> CO₂ + <strong>4</strong> H₂O",
  "chem-aqa-8462-2025-p2f::10.1": "Aluminium is made from metal ore from rock, which is a finite resource, and the ore has to be mined or quarried, which causes noise, dust and damage to the landscape. Wood comes from trees, which are a renewable resource, but growing trees needs land, which reduces land for farming and could lead to deforestation.<br>Wood is much cheaper (relative cost 1 compared with 87 per kg), so more doors can be made for the same cost. Aluminium is stronger (1.3 compared with 1.0), so an aluminium door is less easily damaged. Aluminium does not need any protection because it does not corrode, so it needs less maintenance, whereas wooden doors must be painted regularly to stop them rotting, which costs time and money. At the end of their life both can be reused: aluminium can be melted and recycled into new products, conserving ore and energy, and wood can be shredded into wood chips for fuel, so less goes to landfill.<br>Overall, I think wood is the better material for most doors, because it is renewable and very much cheaper, even though aluminium is stronger and needs less maintenance.",
  "chem-aqa-8462-2025-p2f::10.2": "The aluminium oxide layer acts as a barrier that prevents air (oxygen) and water from reaching the aluminium metal underneath, so the metal cannot corrode.",
